  "question": "Let $$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$$ Verify that $(k+l)A=kA+lA$ for $k=2$ and $l=-3$.",
  "answeroption": "B",
  "explanation": "<b>Left-hand side</b><br>Since $k+l=2-3=-1$, $$(k+l)A=-A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$$<br><b>Right-hand side</b><br>$$2A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\quad -3A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$$<br>Therefore, $$2A+(-3A)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$$<br><b>Scalar multiplication property</b><br>Hence, $(k+l)A=kA+lA$.",
